module stdlib.version

# A counter that only goes forward, for saying WHEN a piece of state was.
#
# What `stamped.fresh` rests on is that a chain never repeats a value: one that
# came round again would let a stale reply find a matching present
# (docs/stale-replies-v0.md §3). That is a property of the HOLDER — a field only
# ever assigned `next` of itself — and this type cannot enforce it.
#
# Sealing the constructor buys something narrower and still worth having: with
# no `Version(n)` from an `Int` and no accessor back to one, a caller cannot put
# an edit count or a line number where a version belongs. It does NOT make a
# version unforgeable, since `origin` and `next` are exported and total.
#
# NOT a `wrap`, though guidelines.md §7 would otherwise ask for one: exporting a
# wrap exports its constructor (spec §5.6.1), leaving `Int` and `Version` one
# coercion apart. The sealed ADT costs one small allocation per step.
export type Version deriving (Eq, Ord, ToString) =
  | Version Int

# Where a chain starts. Two chains are independent, so versions are comparable
# only against others from the same holder.
export fn origin() -> Version = Version(0)

# The next version. O(1).
export fn next(v: Version) -> Version =
  match v with
  | Version n -> Version(n + 1)
